|> Exercise
    handle=_factory_roof_functions_2_
    Find formulas for functions whose graphs look
    like these:

    |> Image
        src=images/svg_ch3_ex_three_factories.svg

    |> Solution

        For the first graph,
        |> del

            the
        an answer is
        $$
        2 \cdot(x/2 - \fl{x/2})
        $$
        which simplifies to
        $$
        x - 2\fl{x/2}
        $$
        because all we have to do is to multiply
        Exercise >>_factory_roof_functions_1_'s formula by $2$.

        For the second graph, an answer is
        $$
        x/3 - \fl{x/3}
        $$
        because the problem is similar to Exercise >>_factory_roof_functions_1_
        except with a factor $3$ horizontal dilation.

        For the third graph, we will first stop to
        find a formula  for the function depicted
        here:

        |> Image
            src=images/svg_ch3_ex_three_factories_sol1.svg

        And that formula is...

        |> Image
            src=images/svg_ch3_ex_three_factories_sol2.svg

        ...iiiiiiiS...
        $$
        (x-1)/3 - \fl{(x-1)/3}
        $$
        as obtained by substituting “$x - 1$”
        (the input, minus $1$) in place of “$x$” in
        “$\,x/3 - \fl{x/3}$”, the formula for the
        second graph. Then we multiply that by $3$
        (to go from 
        |> NoBreak

            “
            |> InlineImage
                src=images/svg_ch3_3_3_grid_1.svg
                width=0.9em
                bottom=0.15em
            ” 
        to 
        |> NoBreak

            “
            |> InlineImage
                src=images/svg_ch3_3_3_grid_2.svg
                width=0.9em
                bottom=0.15em
            ”, 
        namely), meaning that the final answer is
        $$
        3 \cdot ((x-1)/3 - \fl{(x-1)/3})
        $$
        or
        $$
        (x - 1) - 3\fl{(x-1)/3}
        $$
        after simplification. (Or just
        $$
        x - 1 - 3\fl{(x-1)/3}
        $$
        though we personally prefer the previous
        form, it being more “talkative”.)
